For two $k$-element subsets of an $N$-output batch, the largest symmetric difference is $2\min(k,N-k)$. The nominal $2k$ scale is a general upper bound, attained when $2k\le N$. Frozen-cutoff lists can have other sizes, so it is not a universal bound on their churn. An allowance of $\rho$ average membership changes corresponds to $\alpha=\rho/(2k)$. Requiring each replacement to satisfy its own budget instead assigns outputs a vector of replacement costs. Combining those constraints with the average budget no longer justifies sorting a single scalar cost and taking its longest feasible prefix; the optimization and certificate complexity need separate analysis.

